1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:07,500
Why National Archives Still Use Microfilm Preservation Masters. Published practice from major archives and libraries

2
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:19,792
Published preservation policy at major national archives and libraries points to one pattern: microfilm remains the preservation master in most long-term strategies, with digital copies used for access.

3
00:00:19,792 --> 00:00:26,500
Silver-halide film on polyester base, processed and stored to standard, carries an ISO life expectancy rating of LE-500.

4
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:38,084
Longevity that is specified: LE-500 under ISO 18901, with storage to ISO 18911. Readable with light and magnification, with no file format or software to maintain.

5
00:00:38,084 --> 00:00:47,500
Air-gapped by construction: film in a vault is out of reach of ransomware. Legal recognition varies by country, so confirm what applies in yours.

6
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:57,902
1. Camera negative: the master, stored with restricted access. 2. Print master negative: a duplicate used only to make further copies. 3.

7
00:00:57,902 --> 00:01:02,500
Positive service copy: used by researchers and replaced on a cycle.

8
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:10,335
Institution: US National Archives (NARA). Published practice: Agencies must schedule both originals and microforms. Institution: Library of Congress.

9
00:01:10,335 --> 00:01:15,610
Published practice: Large preservation microfilming programme, especially newspapers. Institution: British Library.

10
00:01:15,610 --> 00:01:20,610
Published practice: Masters in the UK, duplicates held abroad. Institution: Smithsonian Institution Archives.

11
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:23,500
Published practice: Duplicates to reduce handling of originals.

12
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:35,539
Is microfilm only for preservation? No. The prevailing approach is hybrid: film as the preservation master, digital for delivery. Why separate master and duplicate sites?

13
00:01:35,539 --> 00:01:39,500
So that no single fire, flood or facility loss can take both.

14
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:43,189
